# Quiet-by-Design at MWAA — the View from the EM Director's Chair

**Author lens:** 25-year airport emergency management director at a top-tier US hub. I have activated the EOC for weather, active shooters, mass casualty, power loss, cyber, IROPS, and pandemic. I read this thesis the way I read every terminal design brief: what does it do to my ability to move information across a terminal at minute three of an activation, when the cell network is saturated, the FIDS network is contested, and 40,000 people need to be told where to go.

---

## 1. Key findings

- **"Quiet" is not the risk. Under-specified quiet is the risk.** A quiet-by-design standard that hardens acoustic performance, PA zoning, STI thresholds, and EM override authority is a net gain for emergency management. A quiet program written as an announcement-reduction policy — without commissioning intelligibility and without a documented EM override — degrades life-safety capability and will fail its first real activation.
- **The regulatory floor is voice, not screens.** 14 CFR § 139.325 requires the airport emergency plan to include a communications network. NFPA 72 Chapter 24 governs the emergency voice/alarm communication system (EVACS), and the accepted intelligibility target is an average Speech Transmission Index ≥ 0.50 in each acoustically distinguishable space (ADS), with ≥ 0.45 at 90% of measurement points. That threshold is the mandatory line for both airports [Source: https://www.faa.gov/documentlibrary/media/advisory_circular/150_5200_31c_consolidated.pdf] [Source: https://www.sdmmag.com/articles/90530-measuring-voice-intelligibility-for-emergency-communications-systems].
- **Every real activation the field learned from routed through voice.** FLL 2017 (active shooter), ATL 2017 (11-hour blackout), the CrowdStrike outage of July 2024, and the DCA 2025 midair collision all produced the same lesson: when digital systems fail, contradict each other, or become untrusted, people obey the voice they can hear. If we cannot produce an intelligible, credible voice message on demand, the terminal manages itself — badly [Source: https://www.policinginstitute.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/Ft.-Lauderdale-Hollywood-International-Airport-AAR.pdf] [Source: https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/morning-mix/wp/2017/12/18/this-place-is-screwed-atlanta-airport-travelers-stranded-in-darkness-as-power-fails-for-nearly-11-hours/].
- **Smartphone-first fallback is an EM failure mode, not a mitigation.** In any incident large enough for an EOC activation, LTE and public Wi-Fi become congested within minutes. Push notifications from airline or airport apps miss non-users, children, low-vision passengers, limited-English-proficiency travelers, and airside staff on radio. ACRP Research Report 201 is explicit that mass notification for people with access and functional needs must be delivered on at least two redundant modes, one of them audible and one of them visual [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/25507/chapter/21].
- **PA zoning is a pro-EM feature — if the EOC controls the override.** European "silent" airports moved to zoned, targeted announcements rather than eliminating them. That model actually helps EM: it lets us push an incident-specific message to Concourse C without triggering all-airport panic. It only helps if the design specifies that the EOC duty officer can seize all zones — publicly, in a documented sequence — during any Level 2+ activation [Source: https://news.schiphol.com/silence-at-amsterdam-airport-schiphol/] [Source: https://crp.trb.org/acrpwebresource2/airport-emergency-communications-for-people-with-disabilities-and-others-with-access-and-functional-needs/].
- **The two MWAA terminals are not symmetric acoustic problems.** IAD's Saarinen main terminal and DCA's Pelli National Hall are high-volume vaulted spaces where reverberation makes STI ≥ 0.50 hard to achieve without embedded absorption and dense loudspeaker coverage. Concourse E — greenfield — is where a common standard actually gets built the first time. The historic buildings are where the standard runs into Section 106 and where it will cost real money to reach compliance.
- **Post-January-29-2025 DCA is not a normal political environment for PA governance.** The Army UH-60 / PSA CRJ700 collision, the temporary flight restrictions, and the family-reunification response set a public expectation that MWAA's mass-communication capability at DCA will be visible and credible during the next event. Any quiet standard adopted now will be read against that backdrop [Source: https://www.ntsb.gov/investigations/Pages/DCA25MA108.aspx].

## 2. Evidence and reasoning

### The regulatory frame — what "quiet" cannot cross

Part 139.325 requires each certificated airport to maintain an Airport Emergency Plan that includes a communications network capable of prompt notification and coordination [Source: https://www.faa.gov/documentlibrary/media/advisory_circular/150_5200_31c_consolidated.pdf]. FAA AC 150/5200-31C — the operative AC — expects airports to integrate NIMS/ICS and to specify how the airport communicates with the public, tenants, and airlines during the response phase [Source: https://www.faa.gov/documentlibrary/media/advisory_circular/150_5200_31c_consolidated.pdf]. Neither document specifies dBA levels, announcement cadence, or STI values; those enter through NFPA 72 as adopted by MWAA's building code and Design Manual [Source: https://www.sdmmag.com/articles/90530-measuring-voice-intelligibility-for-emergency-communications-systems].

NFPA 72's Chapter 24 is the technical spine. The accepted intelligibility criterion — average STI ≥ 0.50 (CIS ≥ 0.70) per ADS, with ≥ 0.45 at 90% of measurement points — is the bar for EVACS in occupied public terminals [Source: https://www.sdmmag.com/articles/90530-measuring-voice-intelligibility-for-emergency-communications-systems] [Source: https://sdifire.com/ensuring-clear-communication/]. This is measured with STIPA under IEC 60268-16, at commissioning and — this is the operational point most designers miss — at recommissioning after any tenant fit-out, ceiling change, or acoustic-treatment substitution. A quiet standard that does not require recommissioning after tenant work will drift out of compliance within a lease cycle [Source: https://www.nti-audio.com/Portals/0/data/en/NTi-Audio-AppNote-STIPA-Measurement.pdf].

The ADA/DOT 49 CFR Part 27 layer is not decorative. Effective communication requires audible information to have a visual counterpart, and assistive listening in assembly areas. ACRP Research Report 201 goes further and treats mass notification for access and functional needs as requiring multiple, redundant, simultaneous modes — this is why "we replaced PA with the app" is not, on its face, an ADA-compliant strategy for emergency messaging [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/25507/chapter/21].

### What real activations taught the field

**FLL January 6, 2017 (active shooter, five killed, six wounded).** The Broward Sheriff's Office / National Policing Institute after-action report is the single most-cited airport document on communications failure in the past decade. Radio channels crashed under load. PA use during the incident was inconsistent, and passengers reported "too little public communication" — in the absence of information, misinformation drove panic and a self-evacuation onto the airfield [Source: https://www.policinginstitute.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/Ft.-Lauderdale-Hollywood-International-Airport-AAR.pdf]. Observation 8 of the AAR specifically flagged use of the PA and fire alarm as areas for corrective action [Source: https://www.policinginstitute.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/Ft.-Lauderdale-Hollywood-International-Airport-AAR.pdf]. Corrective actions across the field afterward included zoned PA with EOC seize authority and pre-scripted incident announcements — not fewer announcements.

**ATL December 17, 2017 (11-hour blackout).** An electrical fire in a Georgia Power tunnel took out both feeders and the redundant backup. The airport was dark for roughly 11 hours; 1,180 flights cancelled that day [Source: https://www.cnn.com/2017/12/17/us/atlanta-airport-power-outage/index.html]. The lesson relevant to a quiet standard is not that PA failed — it did, because the system lost power — but that when PA failed, no other channel replaced it. Passengers on aircraft and in concourses reported being informed by seatmates with phones or not at all [Source: https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/morning-mix/wp/2017/12/18/this-place-is-screwed-atlanta-airport-travelers-stranded-in-darkness-as-power-fails-for-nearly-11-hours/]. Anthony Foxx publicly framed the event as "compounded by confusion and poor communication" [Source: https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/morning-mix/wp/2017/12/18/this-place-is-screwed-atlanta-airport-travelers-stranded-in-darkness-as-power-fails-for-nearly-11-hours/]. Post-event, ATL and peer hubs hardened EVACS backup power, added mobile PA units to EOC caches, and formalized bullhorn-and-runner protocols. A quiet standard has to specify PA survivability — genset-backed, separate riser, at least four hours autonomy in the terminal EVACS.

**CrowdStrike July 19, 2024.** A single Falcon Sensor content update blue-screened Windows hosts globally. Airline DCS, airport FIDS-adjacent systems, and check-in cascaded [Source: https://techcrunch.com/2024/07/19/faulty-crowdstrike-update-causes-major-global-it-outage-taking-out-banks-airlines-and-businesses-globally] [Source: https://simpleflying.com/how-crowdstrike-outage-affected-us-airlines-airports/]. The operational lesson for MWAA: FIDS-first quiet policies assume the FIDS network is up and trustworthy. On July 19 it wasn't. Airports that had retained trained gate agents, staffed information counters, and manual PA scripts contained passenger flow; airports that had thinned staff on the theory that screens would carry the load did not.

**DCA January 29, 2025 (midair collision, 67 fatalities).** The NTSB docket (DCA25MA108) is open; the FAA has published statements confirming ongoing investigation [Source: https://www.ntsb.gov/investigations/Pages/DCA25MA108.aspx] [Source: https://www.faa.gov/newsroom/faa-statements-midair-collision-reagan-washington-national-airport]. From the EM chair, the salient point is not the collision itself but the terminal response: a mass fatality involving arriving passengers pulls family reunification and press management directly through the terminal. That requires the airport's own voice — repeated, calm, in multiple languages, addressable to specific holdrooms — not a hyperlink pushed to an app. Any MWAA quiet standard adopted post-2025 will be scrutinized against this activation.

### The peer benchmarks — what to actually import

Schiphol's "silent airport" model is real, but it is a **zoned, targeted announcement** model, not an elimination model [Source: https://news.schiphol.com/silence-at-amsterdam-airport-schiphol/]. AviaVox and similar vendors sell into that model as automated, multilingual, zone-addressable systems that still produce voice; they reduce operator-initiated gate calls, they do not remove EVACS [Source: https://aviavox.com/]. Copenhagen and Helsinki push the same pattern. London City is small enough that its quiet policy is not transferable to a hub. Dubai is a different regulatory and staffing world. The correct read for MWAA: import the zoning, the automation, the intelligibility discipline, and the announcement governance — do not import the marketing.

ACRP Research Report 170 (Public Notification Programs at Airports) and Synthesis 73 (Emergency Communications Planning for Airports) are the two most direct US operator-side documents on this question and should sit in the MWAA Design Manual's reference list [Source: https://crp.trb.org/acrpwebresource2/airport-emergency-communications-for-people-with-disabilities-and-others-with-access-and-functional-needs/].

## 3. The preparedness case — for and against the thesis

**For — why a quiet-by-design standard strengthens EM.**

A written standard forces four things design projects at MWAA currently negotiate ad hoc: PA zone boundaries; STI performance at commissioning and re-commissioning; EOC seize authority sequence; and tenant/airline PA governance. All four are EM force multipliers. Concourse E — opening autumn 2026 — is the first opportunity to install a fully zoned, STI-verified, EOC-addressable EVACS on greenfield airside space at IAD. If MWAA writes a standard before Concourse E stabilizes, every subsequent Dulles concourse under the $20–22.5 billion expansion inherits it. If MWAA does not, Concourse E's system becomes the de facto standard by default — chosen by whichever design-build team priced the lowest EVACS package. That is not how a hub of MWAA's exposure should set life-safety capability.

The exercise argument is the same. A standard makes the airport tabletop-able. You cannot exercise "the announcement policy varies by terminal and by shift" — you can exercise "override sequence Alpha for Concourse-scope incident, override sequence Bravo for terminal-wide incident."

**Against — where the thesis erodes EM capability if it is written carelessly.**

Three failure modes worry me.

1. **Announcement-reduction becomes an outcome metric.** If MWAA measures success by "fewer announcements," gate agents, TSA, and airlines will comply by suppressing safety-relevant messaging — TSA unattended-bag prompts, airline gate changes, boarding calls for irregular operations. The right metric is intelligibility per required message (STI, STIPA), not announcement count.
2. **App/FIDS becomes the primary channel.** This fails FLL '17, ATL '17, and CrowdStrike simultaneously. A quiet standard that does not explicitly require **three simultaneous channels** (voice + visual/FIDS + staffed/mobile) in defined incident modes is a downgrade masquerading as an upgrade.
3. **EOC override is not written in.** If PA control lives with airline stations, tenants, and terminal ops during normal operations, and there is no rehearsed sequence to seize all zones during an activation, the duty officer discovers this at minute four of a real event. Every EM director has watched this happen once. It should not happen twice.

**Which activations bear on it most directly.** FLL 2017 for what happens when PA is inconsistent under load; ATL 2017 for what happens when PA loses power and nothing replaces it; CrowdStrike 2024 for what happens when digital replaces voice and digital fails; DCA 2025 for the family reunification and public expectation overlay MWAA specifically now sits inside.

## 4. Anchors a strategist can use

1. **NFPA 72 STI ≥ 0.50 at commissioning and recommissioning.** Non-negotiable floor across every ADS at IAD and DCA. Add STIPA verification at any tenant fit-out that touches ceilings, absorption, or PA speaker coverage [Source: https://www.sdmmag.com/articles/90530-measuring-voice-intelligibility-for-emergency-communications-systems].
2. **Concourse E (IAD, opening autumn 2026) as the first-build reference.** ~435,000 sq ft, 14 United gates, greenfield airside. The design decisions frozen for Concourse E become the practical MWAA standard for every subsequent concourse in the $20–22.5B Dulles expansion. Write the standard before those decisions harden.
3. **FLL 2017 AAR — Observation 8 (PA and fire alarm use).** The single most-cited airport EM AAR on public-address failure in an active-shooter response. Cite it as the operational floor: pre-scripted zoned announcements, EOC seize authority, radio interoperability rehearsed against realistic passenger-load scenarios [Source: https://www.policinginstitute.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/Ft.-Lauderdale-Hollywood-International-Airport-AAR.pdf].
4. **ATL 2017 EVACS survivability.** Specify PA and mass-notification survivability under total-loss-of-utility conditions: separate riser, generator-backed, minimum four-hour autonomy, portable EOC PA cache. Historic buildings (IAD Saarinen main terminal; DCA Historic Terminal 1) get compliance paths, not exemptions [Source: https://www.cnn.com/2017/12/17/us/atlanta-airport-power-outage/index.html].
5. **ACRP Research Report 201 as the accessibility spine.** Mandatory redundancy: any emergency message must be delivered simultaneously on voice, visual, and one staffed/wayfinding channel. Optional for routine wayfinding; mandatory for anything the AEP touches [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/25507/chapter/21].

## 5. Bottom line for the Strategist

Adopt the quiet-by-design standard. Do not adopt an "announcement-reduction" policy. Those are not the same document. The first hardens acoustic performance, PA zoning, EOC override, and intelligibility commissioning across both airports — that strengthens the single most undervalued capability MWAA runs, which is the ability to move information across a crowded terminal in the first thirty minutes of a bad day. The second saves airline gate agents some breath and, on the day it matters, leaves my duty officer trying to reach 40,000 people through a push notification. I have watched other airports make that trade. It does not survive contact with a real activation.
